### BookVerse — the circuit breaker.
### Proves the gateway stops calling a failing callee, and starts again once it
### recovers: closed -> open -> half-open -> closed. Also proves what must NOT
### trip it (4xx) and that one callee's outage never touches another's.
###
### Requires the "REST Client" VS Code extension. Run top to bottom.
###
### Breaker config lives in packages/gateway/src/index.ts:
###   minimumRequests 10 · failureRateThreshold 0.5 · windowMs 60_000
###   cooldownMs 50_000 · probeTimeoutMs 2_000
###
### TWO THINGS THAT WILL WASTE YOUR TIME:
###
### 1. Fire section 1 inside 60 seconds. The window is 60s and the floor is 10,
###    so if you wander off mid-section the early failures age out and the total
###    never reaches 10. Normal clicking takes ~15s, so there is room.
###    This is also why the file uses `x-fault-status: 504` and not
###    `x-fault-delay`: a 504 returns in ~10ms and is not retried, so ten of them
###    cost ~160ms. Ten 1.5s delays would eat a quarter of the window.
###
### 2. To reset the breaker at any point: `docker compose restart gateway`.
###    Section 3 otherwise makes you wait out the full 50s cooldown.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123
@missingId = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111

### ============================================================
### SETUP
### ============================================================

### S1. Gateway health — expect 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### S2. Book-service reachable through the gateway — expect 200 with 6 books
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### ============================================================
### 1 · OPEN THE CIRCUIT
###    Ten counted failures inside the 10s window.
###    Each: 504, in single-digit ms. Fire them back to back.
### ============================================================

### 1.1
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.2
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.3
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.4
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.5
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.6
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.7
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.8
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.9
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### 1.10 — the tenth failure trips it.
### Confirm in the gateway log (`npm run logs:gateway`):
###   "circuit breaker"  from:closed  to:open  failures:10  total:10
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 504

### ============================================================
### 2 · THE CIRCUIT IS OPEN
### ============================================================

### 2.1. No fault header at all — book-service is perfectly healthy.
### Expect 503 with `retry-after: 1`, returned in SINGLE-DIGIT ms.
### The speed is the point: 7ms means we never opened a socket. A round trip
### to a healthy book-service is ~90ms, and a failing one costs a full timeout.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### 2.2. PROVE no socket was opened.
### Run `npm run logs:book` and look at the tail: the request above must leave
### NO trace. If book-service logged an "incoming request" for it, the gate is
### running too late in the lifecycle.

### 2.3. One breaker per callee — auth is completely unaffected.
### Expect 200 while book's circuit is open. A single shared breaker would
### fail this, and that is the cascade the pattern exists to prevent.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### ============================================================
### 3 · HALF-OPEN → CLOSED
###    Nothing gets through until the cooldown expires. Then exactly one
###    probe goes, it succeeds, and the circuit closes.
### ============================================================

### 3.1. Immediately after 2.1 — still refused. Expect 503.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### 3.2. ⏱ WAIT 50 SECONDS (cooldownMs), then send this one.
### Expect 200. This request IS the probe: it is the only one allowed
### through, and its success closes the circuit.
### Gateway log should show two more transitions:
###   open -> half-open  (reason: cooldown elapsed)
###   half-open -> closed (reason: probe success)
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### 3.3. Normal traffic again — expect 200.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### ============================================================
### 4 · WHAT MUST NOT TRIP IT
### ============================================================

### 4.1–4.12. A missing book is a 404. The callee is healthy; the request is
### the problem. Fire all twelve — well past minimumRequests — and the circuit
### must stay CLOSED. Counting 4xx here would lock everyone out during a spike
### of bad requests.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.2
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.3
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.4
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.5
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.6
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.7
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.8
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.9
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.10
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.11
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.12
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{missingId}}

### 4.13. Circuit still closed — expect 200, and NO transition in the log.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### ============================================================
### 5 · THE RETRY LADDER (not a breaker test, but it lives on this path)
### ============================================================

### 5.1. A 503 IS retried; a 504 is not. Send this and watch `npm run logs:book`:
### you should count FOUR "Fault status fired" lines for this one request —
### the original plus maxRetriesOn503: 3. Takes ~600ms because of the backoff.
###
### Before 2026-08-25 this produced ELEVEN. The option was set next to
### `upstream`, where it type-checks and is never read.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books
x-fault-status: 503

### 5.2. Note what that means for the breaker: eleven — or four — calls to
### book-service produce exactly ONE recorded outcome. The hooks fire per
### logical request, not per attempt. Ten of THESE would also open the circuit,
### but it takes ~6s of wall clock and flirts with the 10s window.
